#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064003 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de1e3f3a-5a64-573f-8fb1-9952fa36a839" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Sine"}] :question/id #uuid "48ec6930-88b7-5aad-9bb7-c5e23e766f80" :question/math-academy-id "e-470" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{9}{4\\sqrt{1-9x^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^2}}\\textrm{d}u = \\arcsin(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $9x^2 = (3x)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=3x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 3 \\,\\textrm d u=\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{9}{4\\sqrt{1-9{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{9}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(3x)^{2}}}\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{12}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡3x+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064004 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "227abe29-6e31-5afd-b0ca-3b8ed7d8c287" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Tangent"}] :question/id #uuid "3e89a278-54ab-59a6-adb6-38f43c0fbe9a" :question/math-academy-id "e-471" :question/problem "Calculate the integral  $\\displaystyle{\\int \\dfrac{3}{2 + 50x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x }.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{3}{2 + 50x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\int \\dfrac{3}{2(1 + 25x^2)} = \\dfrac 3 2 \\int \\dfrac{1}{1+25x^2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^2}\\textrm{d}u =\\arctan u +C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $25x^2 = (5x)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=5x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=5\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 5\\,\\textrm d u = \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{2+50{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+25{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(5x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡5x+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064005 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2f9806d-3a72-5f0a-a6f4-db8dad8871f6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "cb771ff3-b5a9-5900-913f-df23957760b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-472" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle{\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt 2} \\dfrac{1}{2+x^2}\\, \\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2(1+{x}^{2}/2)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2(1+(x/\\sqrt{2})^{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{1+(x/\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this integral now resembles the basic integral for the inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int\\dfrac{1}{1+u^2}\\,\\textrm d u = \\arctan{u} + C.\n$$\n\nWe make the substitution $u=\\dfrac{x}{\\sqrt 2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\sqrt 2 \\,\\textrm d u = \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064014 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d18bdc7-9387-5b2d-98bd-015fa4a1eba7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Secant"}] :question/id #uuid "56860611-869d-5035-bf8f-f568c774d78d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5014" :question/problem "${\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{5\\, \\textrm{d}x}{|5x|\\sqrt{(5x)^2-1}}  = }$" :question/worked-solution "This resembles the basic integral for $\\textrm{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^2-1}} \\textrm{d}u = \\textrm{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nLet $u = 5x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=5\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n5\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5\\,\\text{d}x}{|5x|\\sqrt{(5x)^{2}-1}} & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}(u)+C \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}(5x)+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287463 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de1e3f3a-5a64-573f-8fb1-9952fa36a839" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db06961f-0dd2-5efc-be06-4e0611964fe0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{36}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(6x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287465} {:answer/id #uuid "5c97499b-3e30-5cef-a80a-c25520c3e23f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(6x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287466} {:answer/id #uuid "cf93e1a6-1bbf-5a81-8c7b-5fe67a0988c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(6x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287467} {:answer/id #uuid "352bf025-f82e-50ad-8c77-25bd6188beb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(6x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287468} {:answer/id #uuid "2a7fbc93-a0d1-54a6-a36d-45226c125827" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db06961f-0dd2-5efc-be06-4e0611964fe0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{36}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(6x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287465} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b9fefbf-0fd1-58ea-a9e5-d4f208246cd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287464}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "71e110b8-dac7-55ea-9c66-c30aaca2c79e" :question/math-academy-id "q-114023" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{1-36{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $36{x}^{2}=(6x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=6x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{6}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{1-36{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{6}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(6x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{6}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(6x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287484 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de1e3f3a-5a64-573f-8fb1-9952fa36a839" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cbfbca95-5141-5253-a86e-9584a7b510a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287486} {:answer/id #uuid "26b0ffcc-656d-5568-abda-f8a626d1b9f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287487} {:answer/id #uuid "86099f70-f073-5f4e-b798-8a5e09c08614" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(3x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287488} {:answer/id #uuid "69dd2123-1bcd-5dc9-81fc-a2533bf9265d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(3x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287489} {:answer/id #uuid "187e2440-fa88-5df3-9582-c6cab739bf44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "187e2440-fa88-5df3-9582-c6cab739bf44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287490} :answer-field/id #uuid "66c7fdf3-5881-51bf-a348-fdc8cc1084f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287485}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34369d01-da71-5054-b6e7-8a4b327608db" :question/math-academy-id "q-114027" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{1-3{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $3{x}^{2}=(\\sqrt{3}x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\sqrt{3}x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{1-3{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{1-(\\sqrt{3}x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{3}}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296126 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de1e3f3a-5a64-573f-8fb1-9952fa36a839" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c321557-b8ab-599d-9637-eded56c518d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(5x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296128} {:answer/id #uuid "4b8a3275-2a3a-5283-92a3-5a3429d9d92b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{25}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296129} {:answer/id #uuid "cda93920-4941-5629-a5a6-dc7511496d5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296130} {:answer/id #uuid "03ebc035-5cdd-5d02-8738-0703aa5b130e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(5x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296131} {:answer/id #uuid "150f57bf-3e36-5795-b8dd-7f8d924537aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "150f57bf-3e36-5795-b8dd-7f8d924537aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296132} :answer-field/id #uuid "99bed4d2-ab4e-5786-80f9-abadf8d7a2fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a98b1cf8-b345-553f-a9f8-49e274be30db" :question/math-academy-id "q-114030" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{25\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/25)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/25}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/5)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{5})}^{2}.$ \n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{5}.$ Differentiating, we get  \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=5\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{5}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/5)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot (5\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296154 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d18bdc7-9387-5b2d-98bd-015fa4a1eba7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Secant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5407476-01e9-587f-9a84-f414950ff22c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{8}\\,\\text{arcsec}(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296156} {:answer/id #uuid "f25c85c8-cf4d-5a21-9f06-5dd705afcaa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{8}}{8}\\,\\text{arcsec}(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296157} {:answer/id #uuid "cf01bf55-d339-5445-b4cb-46298db56091" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(\\sqrt{8}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296158} {:answer/id #uuid "acde7078-c8ce-5bfb-b221-34f38c9d69bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,\\text{arcsec}(\\sqrt{8}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296159} {:answer/id #uuid "1088c4b9-da60-59cd-ab68-0910b963f941" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,\\text{arcsec}(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{8}})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acde7078-c8ce-5bfb-b221-34f38c9d69bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,\\text{arcsec}(\\sqrt{8}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296159} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d2a1f62-e46f-5be2-a82f-01cd0ef0da50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a33f4543-08d0-561d-b9e2-d065f565c833" :question/math-academy-id "q-114056" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{8}{|x|\\sqrt{8{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{8}{|x|\\sqrt{8{x}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{8\\sqrt{8}}{|\\sqrt{8}x|\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{8}x)^{2}-1}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\text{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $\\sqrt{8}x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\sqrt{8}x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{8}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{8}}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8}{|x|\\sqrt{8{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{8\\sqrt{8}}{|\\sqrt{8}x|\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{8}x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{8}\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{8}}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{8}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{8}}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =8\\,\\text{arcsec }u+C \\\\  & =8\\,\\text{arcsec}(\\sqrt{8}x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287512 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2f9806d-3a72-5f0a-a6f4-db8dad8871f6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef617db5-0efb-5b3e-891e-f4998d44f325" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287514} {:answer/id #uuid "954de688-318e-5150-8224-414ec4b16932" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287515} {:answer/id #uuid "5b9b0fb9-7d8c-548e-8b01-c63a6da40345" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287516} {:answer/id #uuid "c9f04de8-eac3-56f3-bf93-acb11e4eecd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287517} {:answer/id #uuid "c38d5173-e586-577f-9b0e-511dd492f8ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287518}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "954de688-318e-5150-8224-414ec4b16932" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287515} :answer-field/id #uuid "41ba77f5-09ca-52c2-aced-ebbb5ccc0204" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287513}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a8a2299c-2891-5f11-90dc-7964d7987169" :question/math-academy-id "q-19393" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/16)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/16}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/4)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{4})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{4}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=4\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{5}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/4)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot (4\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})-5\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287519 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2f9806d-3a72-5f0a-a6f4-db8dad8871f6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28755659-c143-5759-b8da-7a530f51178a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287521} {:answer/id #uuid "95d98d3d-fa42-53cc-b91b-f5819bbbe3ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287522} {:answer/id #uuid "078315a8-7668-5374-8553-215ae24f1dd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287523} {:answer/id #uuid "82d9a02a-225b-58df-9f5a-2b34f14a5198" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287524} {:answer/id #uuid "88c31943-4f4f-5a51-bd33-5fa2048fc973" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287525}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88c31943-4f4f-5a51-bd33-5fa2048fc973" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287525} :answer-field/id #uuid "4150c91b-cc6e-56f7-958b-25e87b0b3a8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287520}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b84d22c-bb4f-5513-8652-92de66383ce5" :question/math-academy-id "q-19396" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1/5}\\frac{20}{3+75{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can write this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1/5}\\frac{20}{3+75{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{20}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1/5}\\frac{1}{1+25{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $25{x}^{2}=(5x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=5x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/5}\\frac{20}{3+75{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{20}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1/5}\\frac{1}{1+(5x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)-\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296182 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "227abe29-6e31-5afd-b0ca-3b8ed7d8c287" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f60cb49-c9f6-581c-977a-79324ee3b3cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296184} {:answer/id #uuid "a4dc3cea-198a-5efa-835e-10ba0dbcf4b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296185} {:answer/id #uuid "3ae0ebc8-2f4e-5603-aa1f-1b2d58ed1c5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296186} {:answer/id #uuid "b674bb5a-c3e8-52f6-9db4-70c1b0c2bade" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296187} {:answer/id #uuid "a7655bba-315f-5d0e-8678-cb96c5e49d16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4dc3cea-198a-5efa-835e-10ba0dbcf4b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296185} :answer-field/id #uuid "a58b9f12-f23a-5c30-94d8-692f32f45430" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "32195101-2029-523b-86e7-1b542af1b41c" :question/math-academy-id "q-49505" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{4\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/4)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}.$ \n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=2\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (2\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064790 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "227abe29-6e31-5afd-b0ca-3b8ed7d8c287" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ebe8b81e-9d29-5285-b27c-39067fdda4e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\arctan (3x) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063251} {:answer/id #uuid "f822bacb-c50f-5046-b9ef-7e15ffe9f297" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\arctan (3x))/(3) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063252} {:answer/id #uuid "186632c2-fc0f-5219-a5b0-d739ed584564" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\arcsin (3x))/(3) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063253} {:answer/id #uuid "38701fa4-6057-5699-a3c6-c3a19a2e3f38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\arcsin (3x) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063254} {:answer/id #uuid "5674be11-46d0-5e8f-bf62-814fc82a205e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\arctan (3x) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063255}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebe8b81e-9d29-5285-b27c-39067fdda4e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\arctan (3x) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063251} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1916893-3a98-5964-91fa-00bf5c3bddad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063250}] :question/id #uuid "5ff7c3e1-6732-5fb3-bcd2-7ed1aeab0712" :question/math-academy-id "q-49540" :question/problem "What is $∫\\frac{6}{1 + 9x^{2}}dx$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291729 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2f9806d-3a72-5f0a-a6f4-db8dad8871f6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fb3e6b9-359d-527e-b381-b4210d9e007d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291731} {:answer/id #uuid "a561a950-d397-503f-92e6-09e6900ad861" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291732} {:answer/id #uuid "3eeafe43-e719-50d8-85c5-4b97ae46b68a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291733} {:answer/id #uuid "a49f572c-a2e7-5ad0-82cc-bbac19d02802" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291734} {:answer/id #uuid "776498a3-8e67-5302-b303-ef2cc1d2f1e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3eeafe43-e719-50d8-85c5-4b97ae46b68a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291733} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d4aa951-af9b-5825-9a3d-c4e6a9a0a712" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291730}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b6c3a048-1e47-59de-8a45-c489c544f459" :question/math-academy-id "q-49546" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-5/2}^{5/2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5/2}^{5/2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}}\\, & ={\\int }_{-5/2}^{5/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{25\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/25)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{-5/2}^{5/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/25}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{-5/2}^{5/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/5)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{5})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{5}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=5\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5/2}^{5/2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}} & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{-5/2}^{5/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/5)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot (5\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u{|}_{-1/2}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064791 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de1e3f3a-5a64-573f-8fb1-9952fa36a839" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eae4fd41-8d6f-56b8-927f-c9985266451f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\arctan (\\frac{x}{7}) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063257} {:answer/id #uuid "1d15a464-b583-5fc8-b316-ebe108dcd317" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\arcsin (\\frac{x}{7}))/(7) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063258} {:answer/id #uuid "2ac1f393-2938-5440-b9aa-dfd8864a8793" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\arctan (7x) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063259} {:answer/id #uuid "5df51dba-e91d-5dcd-9b71-fcec9dc63f3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\arcsin (\\frac{x}{7}))/(3) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063260} {:answer/id #uuid "7d44e4b9-9e22-5ff7-a617-af544b2f2d17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\arcsin (\\frac{x}{7}) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063261}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d44e4b9-9e22-5ff7-a617-af544b2f2d17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\arcsin (\\frac{x}{7}) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063261} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cfb6d85-1b96-50ff-b071-589877187c99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063256}] :question/id #uuid "20c23af2-eaef-5502-8cf2-7e2560ab8597" :question/math-academy-id "q-49547" :question/problem "What is $∫\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{49 - x^{2}}}dx$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287558 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "227abe29-6e31-5afd-b0ca-3b8ed7d8c287" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4c5e771-9cb1-56a1-95ac-c77fe0c7e8f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(8x)}{8}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287560} {:answer/id #uuid "7122d01d-b98c-5c26-9957-977d4cd0e05e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{8})}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287561} {:answer/id #uuid "e0e43244-c07c-535d-a80b-3027cfc6337b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{8})}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287562} {:answer/id #uuid "5fd01d36-e9a0-5dd6-8590-f6961df3ca3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{8})}{8}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287563} {:answer/id #uuid "6f85c4d0-8beb-556a-891b-16513bc22405" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0e43244-c07c-535d-a80b-3027cfc6337b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{8})}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287562} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ad8d76f-0b16-53dc-933f-d51940670705" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287559}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ec0c05df-7d58-5545-ba25-2a23d7c5e6ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-49551" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{2}{64+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{64+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{2}{64\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/64)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{64}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/64}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/8)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{8})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{8}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{8}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=8\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{64+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{32}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/8)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (8\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{8})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064851 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d18bdc7-9387-5b2d-98bd-015fa4a1eba7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Secant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89246796-f084-535b-9bcf-74cb453b66b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(64x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291737} {:answer/id #uuid "71ac8127-e14e-56c4-a515-c5897606da56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\text{arcsec}(8x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291738} {:answer/id #uuid "2a5e0987-5dd6-58f6-afd4-e850f1bd4974" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(8x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291739} {:answer/id #uuid "a256eb39-0a5c-5bdf-b751-4a56f59a8ef3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(4x)}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291740} {:answer/id #uuid "a42e9ab2-f8e1-5790-8b1e-01e07d816281" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(8x)}{8}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a5e0987-5dd6-58f6-afd4-e850f1bd4974" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(8x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291739} :answer-field/id #uuid "b69123c2-7d5f-5bb8-933f-37f2977e820c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c758c6a-6697-5dfb-b64a-bc653c6c226b" :question/math-academy-id "q-69476" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{8\\,\\text{d}x}{|8x|\\sqrt{(8x)^{2}-1}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{8\\,\\text{d}x}{|8x|\\sqrt{(8x)^{2}-1}}=8\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{|8x|\\sqrt{(8x)^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\text{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $8x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=8x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{8}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}8\\int \\frac{1}{|8x|\\sqrt{(8x)^{2}-1}}\\text{d}x & =8\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{8}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec }u+C \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}(8x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291742 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d18bdc7-9387-5b2d-98bd-015fa4a1eba7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Secant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dd4861b-8cb0-523d-bb3c-5e1ce1d22a8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(5x)}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291744} {:answer/id #uuid "b22db8de-8bc0-51eb-a8f8-01c6a1c13c66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{arcsec}(5x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291745} {:answer/id #uuid "adb69dc9-6195-56ff-b839-818aede78a54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(5x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291746} {:answer/id #uuid "2e971d87-82dc-5ce6-b986-b95dcbe2c313" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arctan}(5x)}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291747} {:answer/id #uuid "27341bc8-8f58-540c-aa5e-78dde30499c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{arctan}(5x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1dd4861b-8cb0-523d-bb3c-5e1ce1d22a8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(5x)}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291744} :answer-field/id #uuid "d56cc81d-323e-50ef-89fd-350113ffd0ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291743}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0bbddd4b-1bd5-542e-9d15-dd3b35f6f40f" :question/math-academy-id "q-73718" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{5|x|\\sqrt{25{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{5|x|\\sqrt{25{x}^{2}-1}}=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{|5x|\\sqrt{(5x)^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\text{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $5x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=5x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{5|x|\\sqrt{25{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{|5x|\\sqrt{(5x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\text{arcsec }u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(5x)}{5}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
